IP reassembly — ingress IPv4 fragment table
============================================

Inbound IPv4 fragments are stashed here and reassembled into a
synthetic full-size frame that re-enters `inet_handle_frame` for
normal L4 dispatch. Without this, any peer-side fragmentation
(e.g. a UDP datagram > MTU from a peer that ignored our
advertised MSS) silently drops in L4 demux because the partial
header fails checksum.

Four concurrent datagram assemblies are tracked; each times out
after `IP_REASM_TIMEOUT_MS` ms (RFC 791 — 30 s). Timed-out slots
emit ICMP Time Exceeded (RFC 792 §3.5) toward the source if the
first fragment was seen.

Same `oskit.servers` module as `inet_proto.lsysl`, so sibling
state — the frame pool, link-layer mac, `inet_handle_frame`,
`inet_resolve_mac`, `inet_send_icmp_time_exceeded`,
`inet_nic_send_packet` — resolves without an import. Selftest
helpers in `inet.lsysl` (`inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag`,
`inet_ip_reasm_selftest_run`, `inet_ip_reasm_overlap_test_run`)
reach back here via the same sibling-resolution path.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.services.{monotonic_ms}
    import std.net.{ETHERTYPE_IPV4,
        IP_PROTO_ICMP, ICMP_TIME_EXCEEDED,
        pkt_write_mac, pkt_write_u16, pkt_write_ipv4,
        net_checksum, ipv4, ipv4_octet}


### IP reassembly (ingress fragments)

Inbound IPv4 fragments are stashed here and reassembled into a
synthetic full-size frame that re-enters `inet_handle_frame` for
normal L4 dispatch. Without this, any peer-side fragmentation
(e.g. a UDP datagram > MTU from a peer that ignored our advertised
MSS) silently drops in L4 demux because the partial header fails
checksum.

We support fragments up to `IP_REASM_MAX_DATA` bytes of L4 payload
total — 8 KB, enough for jumbo UDP. Four concurrent datagram
assemblies are tracked; each times out after `IP_REASM_TIMEOUT_MS`
milliseconds (RFC 791 says 30 s = 30000 ms). Timed-out slots
are silently dropped — ICMP Time Exceeded emit is a follow-up.

    val IP_REASM_SLOTS      = 4
    val IP_REASM_MAX_DATA   = 8192
    val IP_REASM_TIMEOUT_MS = 30000
    val IP_REASM_HDR_MAX    = 60

    struct IpReasmSlot
        in_use: int
        poisoned: int
        src_ip: u32
        dst_ip: u32
        ident: int
        proto: int
        deadline: int
        have_first: int
        total_len: int
        ihl_bytes: int
        eth_ip: [74]byte
        payload: [8192]byte
        received: [8192]byte

    var inet_ip_reasm_table: [4]IpReasmSlot
    var inet_ip_reasm_staging: [8320]byte
    var inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent: int = 0


Find the slot matching `(src_ip, dst_ip, ident, proto)`. Returns
slot index or -1.

    inet_ip_reasm_find(src_ip: u32, dst_ip: u32, ident: int, proto: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < IP_REASM_SLOTS
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].src_ip == src_ip
                    if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].dst_ip == dst_ip
                        if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].ident == ident
                            if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].proto == proto
                                return i
            i += 1
        -1


Allocate a free slot, evicting the slot with the earliest
deadline if the table is full.

    inet_ip_reasm_alloc() -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < IP_REASM_SLOTS
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].in_use == 0
                return i
            i += 1
        var best = 0
        var best_dl = inet_ip_reasm_table[0].deadline
        i = 1
        while i < IP_REASM_SLOTS
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].deadline < best_dl
                best = i
                best_dl = inet_ip_reasm_table[i].deadline
            i += 1
        best


Reset slot fields ahead of accepting a new datagram.

    inet_ip_reasm_reset(s: int)
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].in_use = 0
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].poisoned = 0
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].have_first = 0
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].total_len = -1
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].ihl_bytes = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < IP_REASM_MAX_DATA
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].received[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1


Accept one fragment. Returns the assembled frame length (>0) on
completion (written to `inet_ip_reasm_staging`), 0 when more
fragments are needed, -1 on unrecoverable failure (oversize or
malformed).

`eth_ip` points at the start of the inbound Ethernet frame so we
can clone the original ETH+IP header from the first fragment.
`payload` points at the L4 payload (frame + 14 + ihl).

    inet_ip_reasm_accept(eth_ip: *byte, src_ip: u32, dst_ip: u32,
            ident: int, proto: int, mf: int, offset: int,
            ihl_bytes: int, payload: *byte, payload_len: int) -> int
        if offset < 0
            return -1
        if payload_len <= 0
            return -1
        if offset + payload_len > IP_REASM_MAX_DATA
            return -1
        if ihl_bytes > IP_REASM_HDR_MAX
            return -1

        var s = inet_ip_reasm_find(src_ip, dst_ip, ident, proto)
        if s < 0
            s = inet_ip_reasm_alloc()
            inet_ip_reasm_reset(s)
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].in_use = 1
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].src_ip = src_ip
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].dst_ip = dst_ip
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].ident = ident
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].proto = proto

        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + IP_REASM_TIMEOUT_MS

RFC 5722 §4: a fragment that overlaps any previously received
range MUST cause the entire datagram to be dropped. We mark the
slot poisoned and silently drop every subsequent fragment for
the same (src, dst, id, proto) tuple. The slot stays in_use
until its deadline so the attacker can't immediately recycle it
with a fresh-allocation request.

        if inet_ip_reasm_table[s].poisoned != 0
            return 0
        var ov = 0
        while ov < payload_len
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[s].received[offset + ov] != byte(0)
                inet_ip_reasm_table[s].poisoned = 1
                return 0
            ov += 1

        if offset == 0
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].have_first = 1
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].ihl_bytes = ihl_bytes
            var k = 0
            while k < 14 + ihl_bytes
                inet_ip_reasm_table[s].eth_ip[k] = eth_ip[k]
                k += 1

        var i = 0
        while i < payload_len
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].payload[offset + i] = payload[i]
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].received[offset + i] = byte(1)
            i += 1

        if mf == 0
            inet_ip_reasm_table[s].total_len = offset + payload_len

        if inet_ip_reasm_table[s].have_first == 0
            return 0
        val tl = inet_ip_reasm_table[s].total_len
        if tl <= 0
            return 0
        var j = 0
        while j < tl
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[s].received[j] == byte(0)
                return 0
            j += 1

Assemble the synthetic frame: ETH + (patched IP header) +
contiguous payload. We patch IP total_length, clear
flags-and-fragment-offset, and recompute the IP header checksum;
the L4 checksum is left as-is since the peer computed it over the
full datagram before fragmenting.

        val hdr = inet_ip_reasm_table[s].ihl_bytes
        var w = 0
        while w < 14 + hdr
            inet_ip_reasm_staging[w] = inet_ip_reasm_table[s].eth_ip[w]
            w += 1
        val ip_total = hdr + tl
        pkt_write_u16(inet_ip_reasm_staging, 14 + 2, ip_total)
        pkt_write_u16(inet_ip_reasm_staging, 14 + 6, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(inet_ip_reasm_staging, 14 + 10, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(inet_ip_reasm_staging, 14 + 10,
            net_checksum(inet_ip_reasm_staging + 14, hdr))
        var p = 0
        while p < tl
            inet_ip_reasm_staging[14 + hdr + p] = inet_ip_reasm_table[s].payload[p]
            p += 1
        inet_ip_reasm_table[s].in_use = 0
        return 14 + hdr + tl


Send ICMP Type 11 Code 1 (Time Exceeded / Fragment reassembly time
exceeded) per RFC 792 §3.5 toward the original sender of `eth_ip`.
The ICMP body carries the original IP header (`ihl_bytes`) plus
the first 8 bytes of L4 payload — for fragmented datagrams the
caller must supply this from the slot's first-fragment payload,
since later fragments don't include those bytes.

Bumps `inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent` on every call (whether the
NIC send actually succeeds or not) so test infrastructure can
verify the emit attempt without depending on slirp / ARP state.

Returns 0 on emit, -1 if MAC unknown or peer ARP cache miss.

    inet_send_icmp_time_exceeded(eth_ip: *byte, ihl_bytes: int,
                                  payload8: *byte) -> int
        inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent += 1
        if inet_our_mac_known == 0
            return -1
        val peer_ip = ipv4(int(eth_ip[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(eth_ip[14 + 13]) & 0xff,
                           int(eth_ip[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(eth_ip[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
        var next_hop_mac: [6]byte
        if inet_resolve_mac(peer_ip, next_hop_mac) == 0
            return -1
        val fidx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val frame = inet_frame_buf(fidx)
        val icmp_body = ihl_bytes + 8
        val icmp_len = 8 + icmp_body
        val ip_total = 20 + icmp_len
        val total = 14 + ip_total
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 0, next_hop_mac)
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 6, inet_our_mac)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 12, ETHERTYPE_IPV4)
        frame[14] = byte(0x45)
        frame[15] = byte(0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 16, ip_total)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 18, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 20, 0x4000)
        frame[22] = byte(64)
        frame[23] = byte(IP_PROTO_ICMP)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 26,
            INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 30,
            ipv4_octet(peer_ip, 0), ipv4_octet(peer_ip, 1),
            ipv4_octet(peer_ip, 2), ipv4_octet(peer_ip, 3))
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))
        val icmp_off = 14 + 20
        frame[icmp_off + 0] = byte(11)         // ICMP_TIME_EXCEEDED
        frame[icmp_off + 1] = byte(1)          // code 1: frag reassembly
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 2, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 4, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 6, 0)
        // Body: original IP header + 8 bytes of original L4 payload.
        var i = 0
        while i < ihl_bytes
            frame[icmp_off + 8 + i] = eth_ip[14 + i]
            i += 1
        var j = 0
        while j < 8
            frame[icmp_off + 8 + ihl_bytes + j] = payload8[j]
            j += 1
        pkt_write_u16(frame, icmp_off + 2,
            net_checksum(frame + icmp_off, icmp_len))
        inet_nic_send_packet(frame, total)
        inet_frame_release(fidx)
        0


Force-expire a reassembly slot's deadline so the next scan sweep
clears it. Used by test infrastructure (inet.lsysl can't write
sibling-file vars directly per `feedback_sysl_module_split.md`).

    inet_ip_reasm_force_expire(s: int)
        if s >= 0
            if s < IP_REASM_SLOTS
                inet_ip_reasm_table[s].deadline = 0


Drop slots whose deadline has passed. Called once per timer tick
from the inet main loop alongside `inet_tcp_scan_timers`. Emits
ICMP Time Exceeded (RFC 792 §3.5) toward the source if we ever
saw the first fragment — without it we don't have an IP header
to embed and skipping the emit is mandatory.

    inet_ip_reasm_scan_timeouts()
        val now = int(monotonic_ms())
        var i = 0
        while i < IP_REASM_SLOTS
            if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].in_use != 0
                if now >= inet_ip_reasm_table[i].deadline
                    if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].have_first != 0
                        if inet_ip_reasm_table[i].poisoned == 0
                            inet_send_icmp_time_exceeded(
                                inet_ip_reasm_table[i].eth_ip,
                                inet_ip_reasm_table[i].ihl_bytes,
                                inet_ip_reasm_table[i].payload)
                    inet_ip_reasm_table[i].in_use = 0
            i += 1

